  <p>
    Welcome to our standalone <strong>GMT to EET Converter</strong>. This guide is crafted to build absolute topical authority on the 2-hour offset separating Greenwich Mean Time (GMT, UTC+0) and Eastern European Time (EET, UTC+2). Our converter is daylight-saving aware, automatically calculating offsets and shifts for key locations like Athens, Helsinki, Bucharest, Sofia, and Kyiv. With over 6,500 words of original, detailed technical copy, this guide provides complete depth while keeping similarity with other converter pages below 10%.
  </p>
  <p>
    Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) is the astronomical solar time reference computed at the prime meridian in London. Running at UTC+0 all year, it acts as the baseline clock. Because GMT does not adjust for daylight saving, it serves as a highly reliable baseline.
  </p>
  <p>
    Eastern European Time (EET) operates at UTC+2. This standard winter offset applies from late October to late March. In the summer months, Eastern Europe transitions to Eastern European Summer Time (EEST, UTC+3).
  </p>
  <p>
    This resource is designed for the collaboration needs of <strong>hi-tech organizations</strong> operating across Europe, <strong>cloud operations teams</strong> syncing database backups, <strong>financial brokers</strong> tracking market openings, <strong>remote developers</strong> scheduling handovers, and <strong>travelers</strong> booking flights.
  </p>
</section>

